docs/src/user/en\basics\keybindings.md:1:# Keybindings
docs/src/user/en\basics\keybindings.md:3:Every key WowVision uses can be changed. The keybinding screen lists each action, the keys it has, and lets you add keys, remove keys and move a key from one action to another. This page explains how to use it and what the messages mean. The complete list of actions and their default keys is in the [keybinding reference](../reference/keybindings.md).
docs/src/user/en\basics\keybindings.md:5:## Opening the keybinding screen
docs/src/user/en\basics\keybindings.md:9:3. Press Down to Bindings and press Enter.
docs/src/user/en\basics\keybindings.md:11:You land on the first binding and hear, for example, "Bindings, List, Up, Bar, Up Arrow, Button, 1 of 2". Escape goes back to the UI menu.
docs/src/user/en\basics\keybindings.md:52:1. Open the keybinding screen as above.
docs/src/user/en\basics\keybindings.md:72:| "This input already exists for this binding." | The action already has this key. | Nothing changed. |
docs/src/user/en\basics\keybindings.md:74:| "Cannot replace this binding as it requires at least one input." | The other action is a vital one and this is its only key. | Give that action another key first, then try again. |
docs/src/user/en\basics\keybindings.md:78:WowVision only checks keys against its own actions. The game's own key bindings are not checked, and while WowVision's key is active it takes precedence. Pick keys you do not need for something else.
docs/src/user/en\basics\keybindings.md:82:Vital actions keep at least one key at all times, so you can never lock yourself out of the interface. Trying to remove the last key of one gives "Cannot remove; Binding must have at least one input." You can still add another key and then remove the old one.
docs/src/user/en\basics\keybindings.md:108:Key bindings are account-wide, so a key you change on one character changes on all of them. To give one character its own keys, go to Bindings in the UI menu, press Shift+F10, choose Scope, then your character's name. That character gets a copy of the current keys, all of them at once; choose Global to go back to the shared keys. There is no "reset to defaults" button; to restore a default key, add it back from the [keybinding reference](../reference/keybindings.md).
docs/src/user/en\basics\keybindings.md:112:If you run the addon Sku as well, WowVision does not take a few keys that Sku also uses, so the two do not fight. These are the four tooltip line keys (Shift+Up, Shift+Down, Shift+Left, Shift+Right), the soft targeting keys (Shift+I, Shift+P, Shift+O) and the five Target Focus keys. Other commands that Sku also has, such as /pquit, /dquit and /focus1 to /focus5, are likewise left to Sku. The keys still show in the keybinding screen, but they do nothing while Sku is loaded. In the same way, WowVision leaves many game windows (bags, merchant, gossip, mail, trade, quest and others) to Sku.
docs/src/user/en\basics\keybindings.md:116:When the game's gamepad options are on, each row of the keybinding screen also shows the controller buttons assigned to the action, followed by Add Controller Button. They work like keys:
docs/src/user/en\basics\keybindings.md:128:- [Keybinding reference](../reference/keybindings.md)
docs/src/user/en\basics\speech.md:57:- Alerts can interrupt too. A TTS alert with Priority Message (interrupts) switched on stops the current speech before it speaks, as the Target Change alert does. See [Alerts](./alerts.md).
docs/src/user/en\basics\speech.md:79:Every TTS alert output has its own Voice setting. Until you choose something there, it simply follows Speech Voice, and it shows that voice as its value. Pick another voice and that alert always uses it. This lets you, for instance, hear target changes in one voice and chat in another.
docs/src/user/en\world\index.md:8:- [Navigation and Routes](navigation.md) — the navigation window, routes, waypoints and beacons
docs/src/user/en\world\combat.md:9:WoW does not let addons change key bindings or use many of its functions during combat. WowVision therefore steps aside the moment a fight begins:
docs/src/user/en\world\combat.md:23:- Storing a virtual focus with /focus1 to /focus5: refused with the message "Cannot set virtual focus while in combat."
docs/src/user/en\world\combat.md:24:- Capturing a new key in the keybinding window: WowVision says "Not in combat".
docs/src/user/en\world\combat.md:31:- Your target's health cannot be read, so there is no target health monitor. Use the game's own Combat Audio Alerts instead, in its Accessibility options: they can say your target's health, in combat too. See [Targeting](targeting.md).
docs/src/user/en\world\combat.md:58:Each has a TTS Alert output with the usual settings (see [Alerts](../basics/alerts.md)): you can turn the whole alert off with Enabled, turn off the spoken output, or choose a different voice for it. Only the spoken output exists for these two alerts.
docs/src/user/en\world\combat.md:71:| Enemy Count Alert, TTS Alert | On | Speaks the number |
docs/src/user/en\world\combat.md:124:| Announce Errors, TTS Alert | On | How the messages listed under "Always spoken" below are spoken |
docs/src/user/en\world\combat.md:125:| Repeat Delay | 2 | Seconds, 0 to 30. The same message is spoken at most once in this time. This stops "Out of range" from stuttering while you keep a key pressed. 0 switches the delay off |
docs/src/user/en\world\combat.md:128:Holding a key down still repeats the message, once every Repeat Delay seconds. The delay counts from the last time the message was actually spoken.
docs/src/user/en\world\combat.md:139:| TTS Alert | On | Speaks the message. Has the usual Priority Message (interrupts) and Voice settings |
docs/src/user/en\world\navigation.md:1:# Navigation: Maps, Routes and Beacons
docs/src/user/en\world\navigation.md:3:WowVision can guide you to a place on foot. You pick where you start and where you want to go in the navigation window (F10), WowVision works out a route along known paths, and then an audio beacon leads you from waypoint to waypoint until you arrive. This page covers the navigation window, the beacon and all the keys you use while walking a route.
docs/src/user/en\world\navigation.md:10:4. WowVision says how many waypoints the route has and how long it is, and a beacon starts to ping.
docs/src/user/en\world\navigation.md:11:5. Turn towards the beacon, walk, and listen. Each waypoint you reach plays a short sound and the beacon moves on to the next one. At the end you hear "Arrived" and the name of the place.
docs/src/user/en\world\navigation.md:13:You can also set a straight-line beacon with no route at all, from the Direct checkbox in the window, from the scanner, or with the /beacon command.
docs/src/user/en\world\navigation.md:24:| Mists of Pandaria and Retail | No map data is included. The navigation window opens but its lists are empty. Straight-line beacons, /beacon, the distance announcement and every other key on this page still work. |
docs/src/user/en\world\navigation.md:26:Both systems use the same window, the same keys and the same beacon, so this page applies to both, and says so where they differ. What is special about each one is in [Classic routes](#classic-routes) further down and on the [WowNav](./wownav.md) page.
docs/src/user/en\world\navigation.md:59:Tick Direct (press Enter on the checkbox) if you do not want a route. The entry points disappear, and Enter on a destination sets a straight beacon to exactly that point. You hear, for example, "Direct: Innkeeper Allison (Innkeeper), 120 yards", and on arrival "Arrived" followed by the name. Use this when you are already close, when you are inside a building the network does not know, or when you want to go somewhere in a straight line.
docs/src/user/en\world\navigation.md:80:| Enter | Close the window and start the route (or the straight beacon, if Direct is on). |
docs/src/user/en\world\navigation.md:88:In the scanner ([Scanner](./scanner.md)), Enter on a quest, an NPC or any other placed entry opens the navigation window already aimed at it. The closest entry point and the scanner's entry as destination are both selected, and the focus lands on the destination. Press Enter to route there; if you want a different entry point, press Shift+Tab twice first. Without map data for the continent the scanner sets a straight beacon directly and does not open the window.
docs/src/user/en\world\navigation.md:94:When the route starts, WowVision speaks the summary and the beacon begins to ping. Waypoints are not read out one by one. Instead you hear them:
docs/src/user/en\world\navigation.md:96:- The beacon pings towards the current waypoint. The ping changes with the direction and the distance to the waypoint. It pings faster the more directly you face the waypoint, about every 0.7 seconds when off course and about every 0.3 seconds when you are aimed at it. Distance is heard in steps of about 6 yards and works out to roughly 180 yards.
docs/src/user/en\world\navigation.md:100:- Beacon stopped is spoken when you cancel.
docs/src/user/en\world\navigation.md:104:### Beacon sounds
docs/src/user/en\world\navigation.md:106:All beacon sounds are one alert called Beacon in the Maps module (WowVision menu, Navigation, Maps, Beacon). The alert and each of its four sounds has its own Enabled switch and its own sound, so each cue can be turned off or changed on its own. See [Alerts](../basics/alerts.md) for how alerts work.
docs/src/user/en\world\navigation.md:110:| Beacon Sound | Probe (Mid) | The directional ping towards the current waypoint. |
docs/src/user/en\world\navigation.md:111:| Beacon Aligned | clack | You turned to face the waypoint. |
docs/src/user/en\world\navigation.md:112:| Beacon Off Course | click | You turned away from the waypoint. |
docs/src/user/en\world\navigation.md:115:For Beacon Sound you choose one of the beacon sets: Notification, Probe (Deep), Probe (Mid), Steel Gong and Beacon 6 (Sku's beacon 6). Moving onto an entry in the picker plays a sample. For the other three you pick any sound from the sound list.
docs/src/user/en\world\navigation.md:119:| Key | Binding name | What it does |
docs/src/user/en\world\navigation.md:128:| Shift+F12 | Cancel Navigation | Stops the route or beacon. |
docs/src/user/en\world\navigation.md:131:All of these can be changed in the keybinding window (WowVision menu, UI, Bindings); see [Keybindings](../basics/keybindings.md). The full list is in the [binding reference](../reference/keybindings.md).
docs/src/user/en\world\navigation.md:165:- Any other shortcut that uses Ctrl+Alt will also trigger the announcement. If that bothers you, turn off the Announce Distance with Alt Gr setting and bind the Announce Distance binding to a key you do not use otherwise.
docs/src/user/en\world\navigation.md:169:When you have wandered off, or want to skip a waypoint you cannot reach, press Ctrl+W to move the beacon to the next waypoint or Ctrl+S to go back one. The step plays the waypoint-reached sound and speaks how many waypoints remain, current one included, for example "4 remaining". At the ends you hear End of route or Start of route; only walking to the last waypoint finishes the route. Without a route you hear No active waypoint.
docs/src/user/en\world\navigation.md:177:Shift+F12 stops the route or straight beacon: you hear Beacon stopped and the pings end. If nothing is running you hear No active waypoint. /beacon stop does the same.
docs/src/user/en\world\navigation.md:181:The Route to Your Target binding takes the creature or group member you are targeting as the destination. It is unbound by default; bind it in the keybinding window. See also [Targeting](./targeting.md).
docs/src/user/en\world\navigation.md:203:Two bindings, both unbound by default, lead you to the nearest unvisited destination with the same name as the last one you picked:
docs/src/user/en\world\navigation.md:205:| Binding | What it does |
docs/src/user/en\world\navigation.md:208:| Direct to the Nearest Unvisited of the Same Name | Sets a straight beacon there. |
docs/src/user/en\world\navigation.md:214:## Straight beacons and the /beacon command
docs/src/user/en\world\navigation.md:216:You can set a beacon without the window. Type these commands in the chat box:
docs/src/user/en\world\navigation.md:220:| /beacon | Speaks your position in map percentages, such as "50.5 30.4", and, if a beacon is active, its distance and angle. |
docs/src/user/en\world\navigation.md:221:| /beacon 50.5 30.4 | Straight beacon to map coordinates on your current zone map: the numbers quest guides give you. You hear "Beacon set" and the distance. |
docs/src/user/en\world\navigation.md:222:| /beacon to bank | Routes to the nearest known place whose name contains "bank". You hear its name, then the route summary. You do not choose an entry point: WowNav uses the one F10 would preselect, and with classic routes the router picks the best of the five nearest waypoints. |
docs/src/user/en\world\navigation.md:223:| /beacon world x y | Straight beacon to raw world coordinates. |
docs/src/user/en\world\navigation.md:224:| /beacon stop | Same as Shift+F12. |
docs/src/user/en\world\navigation.md:226:With /beacon to, an unknown name gives "No route found" and the name. A beacon from /beacon with coordinates ends with "Arrived" when you get close enough, like any route.
docs/src/user/en\world\navigation.md:252:| Enabled | On | Turns the whole Maps module off, including the keys and /beacon. |
docs/src/user/en\world\navigation.md:257:| Announce Distance with Alt Gr | On | Whether Alt Gr and Ctrl+Alt speak the distance. The Announce Distance binding works either way. |
docs/src/user/en\world\navigation.md:258:| Beacon | (see above) | The beacon alert and its four sounds: Beacon Sound, Beacon Aligned, Beacon Off Course, Waypoint Reached. Each has Enabled and a sound choice. |
docs/src/user/en\world\navigation.md:282:5. Press I to turn to the first waypoint, and walk. When you hear the beacon ping faster, you are facing the right way. Press Alt Gr at any time to hear how far the current waypoint is.
docs/src/user/en\world\navigation.md:291:4. Walk the route as above. If the NPC is not on the path network, the final stretch is not a known path, so use the beacon and Alt Gr. With classic routes the summary says so ("last 25 yards direct"), and WowNav tells you at the end of the network how far the NPC still is.
docs/src/user/en\world\navigation.md:305:- [Alerts](../basics/alerts.md): how to change the beacon sounds.
docs/src/user/en\world\wownav.md:11:WowNav is on by default. Every route request goes to it: the navigation window (F10), the scanner's Enter, /beacon to, the route to your target, the corpse run and the visited-places keys. The classic routes are not used while it is on.
docs/src/user/en\world\wownav.md:39:On a map the data does not know, F10 lists nothing and /beacon to answers "No route found". The scanner's Enter then sets a straight beacon, as it does without map data. If the place list of a map cannot be read at all, you hear "WowNav catalogue not usable" followed by a reason; include it in a bug report.
docs/src/user/en\world\wownav.md:78:- With Direct on, no destination is dropped as unreachable, because a straight beacon needs no route.
docs/src/user/en\world\wownav.md:103:The beacon, its sounds, Ctrl+W, Ctrl+S, Shift+F12, Alt Gr and I all work as in [Navigation](./navigation.md). The differences:
docs/src/user/en\world\wownav.md:108:- Shift+F12 ends everything WowNav does: the search, the route, a transit that waits for its far end, the dock guidance and the beacon at a fishing spot.
docs/src/user/en\world\wownav.md:109:- /beacon to name takes the nearest place with that name, from WowNav's list, with the entry point F10 would preselect.
docs/src/user/en\world\wownav.md:119:| "One waypoint back" | You were pushed off sideways at a narrow gate, so the beacon went back one waypoint. |
