docs/src/user/en\world\quests.md:119:| TTS Alert, Voice | Follows your Speech Voice | Choose a voice for this alert alone. |
docs/src/user/en\windows\bags-and-bank.md:175:The bags and the bank have one setting, Bag Shape, described above. Like every window, the bags have an Enabled switch in the WowVision menu, under Windows, then Containers. They have no alerts and no key bindings of their own. The keys on this page are the shared window keys, which can be changed in the keybinding window. See [Keybindings](../basics/keybindings.md).
docs/src/user/en\windows\chat.md:35:| TTS Alert | On | A group with three settings: Enabled (speak the message), Priority Message (interrupts) (when on, the message interrupts whatever is being said instead of waiting its turn; off by default) and Voice (which voice speaks it; by default it follows your speech voice). |
docs/src/user/en\windows\chat.md:217:| One entry per open chat tab, named like the tab | see above | The tab's alert: Enabled, TTS Alert, Sound Alert and Speak Messages (When the Tab Is Shown by default). |
docs/src/user/en\windows\chat.md:238:The three chat-specific keys (Previous Link in Message, Next Link in Message, Shift Click) and Chat can be changed in the keybinding window (see [Keybindings](../basics/keybindings.md)). They only hold their keys while the message list has the focus.
docs/src/user/en\world\targeting.md:3:WowVision tells you what you target, lets you keep up to five "virtual focus" targets on keys, can switch the game's soft targeting on and off, and opens the target's menu with a single key. This page covers all of it: the announcements, the keys, and the settings.
docs/src/user/en\world\targeting.md:9:| Key | Binding name | What it does |
docs/src/user/en\world\targeting.md:15:| unbound by default | Target Focus 1 to Target Focus 5 | Targets the unit you stored in that virtual focus slot |
docs/src/user/en\world\targeting.md:17:You can change or add keys in the keybinding window (WowVision menu, UI, Bindings); see [Keybindings](../basics/keybindings.md). The complete list of all keys is in the [keybinding reference](../reference/keybindings.md).
docs/src/user/en\world\targeting.md:43:| TTS Alert | On | Speaks the target. Its Priority Message (interrupts) setting is on by default, and Voice lets you pick a voice just for this alert |
docs/src/user/en\world\targeting.md:52:| TTS Alert | On | Speaks the percentage, for example "85%" |
docs/src/user/en\world\targeting.md:57:(Retail and WoW: Forever) There is no Health Monitor here, because the game hides a target's health from addons in combat, just when you need it. Use the game's own Combat Audio Alerts instead, in its Accessibility options: they can say your target's health, in combat too.
docs/src/user/en\world\targeting.md:72:| TTS Alert | Off | Speaks "Controlled" or "Free" instead of, or in addition to, the sounds |
docs/src/user/en\world\targeting.md:102:Each type is an alert in the Targeting menu named Soft Target Enemy, Soft Target Friend and Soft Target Interact. The alert is off by default; its Enabled setting is what the key flips. Under it you find TTS Alert (spoken, interrupting by default) and Sound Alert, both on by default once the alert is on. You can pick a different sound with the Sound setting of the sound output.
docs/src/user/en\world\targeting.md:143:## Virtual focus: five stored targets
docs/src/user/en\world\targeting.md:145:The virtual focus works like the Sku feature of the same name. You store up to five targets by name and bring each back with its own key. It is independent of the game's own focus target.
docs/src/user/en\world\targeting.md:147:The module is called Virtual Focus and sits under Targeting in the menu. It has no settings of its own.
docs/src/user/en\world\targeting.md:154:- /focus2 Some Name stores that name in slot 2, whether or not you target anything.
docs/src/user/en\world\targeting.md:156:WowVision answers in chat, for example "Focus 1 set to Hogger.". Storing is not possible during combat; WowVision then prints "Cannot set virtual focus while in combat." Do it before the pull.
docs/src/user/en\world\targeting.md:160:Bind a key to Target Focus 1 to Target Focus 5 (they are unbound by default; see [Keybindings](../basics/keybindings.md)). Pressing it targets the stored name, exactly like typing /target with that name. Pressing the key also works in combat.
docs/src/user/en\world\targeting.md:162:Because the focus is a name, the game targets the nearest unit with that name. Two monsters with the same name cannot be told apart. Virtual focus slots are not saved: after /reload or a new login the slots are empty and must be set again.
docs/src/user/en\world\targeting.md:167:2. Target the second boss and type /focus2, then the third and /focus3.
docs/src/user/en\world\targeting.md:168:3. In the keybinding window assign Alt+1, Alt+2 and Alt+3 to Target Focus 1, 2 and 3.
docs/src/user/en\windows\collections.md:19:On WoW: Forever the game has a separate key binding for each tab (mounts, pets, toy box, heirlooms, appearances). Each opens the journal directly on that tab. You can set them in the game's key bindings screen, outside WowVision.
docs/src/user/en\reference\commands.md:10:- Global commands are their own slash word, for example /beacon or /pquit.
docs/src/user/en\reference\commands.md:26:| /wv bind | Open the keybinding window | all |
docs/src/user/en\reference\commands.md:29:| /beacon | Sets a beacon, routes to a named place, or reads your position | all |
docs/src/user/en\reference\commands.md:30:| /focus1 to /focus5 | Sets a virtual focus target | all, not with Sku |
docs/src/user/en\reference\commands.md:73:Opens the keybinding window directly, the same screen as WowVision menu (Shift+F1), UI, Bindings. Escape closes it. See [Keybindings](../basics/keybindings.md).
docs/src/user/en\reference\commands.md:83:### /beacon
docs/src/user/en\reference\commands.md:85:Sets a straight-line beacon, routes to a named place, or tells you where you are. The beacon then plays its sound and speaks as you walk. This is the same beacon the navigation window uses. See [Navigation](../world/navigation.md).
docs/src/user/en\reference\commands.md:89:| /beacon | Speaks your position on the zone map (for example "50.5 30.4") and, if a beacon is active, its distance and the degrees to turn |
docs/src/user/en\reference\commands.md:90:| /beacon x y | Sets a beacon to map coordinates on the current zone map: the percentages quest guides give, like 50.5 30.4 |
docs/src/user/en\reference\commands.md:91:| /beacon world x y | Sets a beacon to raw world coordinates |
docs/src/user/en\reference\commands.md:92:| /beacon to name | Routes to the nearest known place whose name contains the text, using the map data. Say a part of a name, such as "mailbox". |
docs/src/user/en\reference\commands.md:93:| /beacon stop | Stops the beacon or route, the same as Shift+F12 |
docs/src/user/en\reference\commands.md:95:Examples: /beacon 50.5 30.4 says "Beacon set 245 yards" and starts a straight-line beacon to that spot; it does not open the navigation window. /beacon to innkeeper speaks the place it found and starts a route to it, or says "No route found innkeeper". /beacon 12 with only one number says "Usage beacon x y".
docs/src/user/en\reference\commands.md:97:Versions: all. /beacon to needs map data for the continent you are on; see [Navigation](../world/navigation.md).
docs/src/user/en\reference\commands.md:101:WoW only supports one focus target. WowVision adds five virtual ones, which you can then target with the Target Focus 1 to Target Focus 5 bindings (unbound by default).
docs/src/user/en\reference\commands.md:108:With a target, WowVision remembers the target's name, so two creatures with the same name cannot be told apart. It prints "Focus 1 set to Name." It does not work in combat and says "Cannot set virtual focus while in combat." Versions: all. When Sku is loaded, the command is not registered. See [Targeting](../world/targeting.md).
docs/src/user/en\reference\commands.md:124:Example: /disableaddon sku. The name sku is special: it switches the Sku addon together with its audio data and beacon addons, so nothing is left half-loaded. /enableaddon sku turns them all back on.
docs/src/user/en\windows\loot-trade-mail.md:19:| Key | Binding name | What it does |
docs/src/user/en\windows\loot-trade-mail.md:27:All of these can be changed in the keybinding window. See [Keybindings](../basics/keybindings.md).
docs/src/user/en\windows\loot-trade-mail.md:76:1. Tabs: Inbox and Send Mail. The one you are on says "selected". Left and Right move between them, and Enter switches.
docs/src/user/en\windows\loot-trade-mail.md:77:2. Open All: a button that takes everything from your inbox, when the game shows it.
docs/src/user/en\windows\loot-trade-mail.md:78:3. Inbox: your letters on this page.
docs/src/user/en\windows\loot-trade-mail.md:83:### The inbox
docs/src/user/en\windows\loot-trade-mail.md:90:The inbox does not say whether a letter has gold or items. You find that out when you open it. An empty inbox says "Empty".
docs/src/user/en\windows\loot-trade-mail.md:112:3. Down to the money attachment and press Enter. The gold is in your purse.
docs/src/user/en\windows\loot-trade-mail.md:147:None of these windows has settings or alerts of its own. The roll keys can be rebound in the keybinding window. See [Keybindings](../basics/keybindings.md). In the WowVision menu, under Windows, the Loot, Trade and Mail entries each have just an Enabled switch. Switching Loot off turns the roll keys off.
docs/src/user/en\information\buffers.md:30:These keys work anywhere in the game. Each one interrupts whatever WowVision is saying, so you can press them quickly. You can change the keys in the keybinding window, see [Keybindings](../basics/keybindings.md).
docs/src/user/en\information\group-health.md:65:To change the key, open the WowVision menu, go to UI, then Bindings, or type `/wv bind`. See [Keybindings](../basics/keybindings.md).
docs/src/user/en\windows\overview.md:55:Windows that every version reads: bags and bank, merchants, NPC dialogs, the quest giver window, flight masters, trainers, trade, the mailbox inbox, books and letters, the game's confirmation popups, ready check and role check, guild invitations, the game menu and options, macros and the color picker. Beyond that, each version has its own set:
docs/src/user/en\information\monitors.md:15:- Each state of a rule has an alert with a Sound Alert and a TTS Alert output.
docs/src/user/en\information\monitors.md:52:8. Open the state you care about (Applied, Missing, Ready, and so on), then open Sound Alert or TTS Alert and turn it on. See [Alerts](#state-alerts-and-their-outputs) below.
docs/src/user/en\information\monitors.md:85:| TTS Alert | Off | Speaks a message. Opens its own screen |
docs/src/user/en\information\monitors.md:87:Turn on the output you want, and leave the state's own Enabled on. At the moment that switch does nothing for the states of any rule: only the Enabled switch of the Sound Alert or TTS Alert inside decides whether something plays. The only alerts that obey the state's own Enabled are Charge Gained and Charge Lost of the Cooldown rule.
docs/src/user/en\information\monitors.md:96:The TTS Alert screen has:
docs/src/user/en\information\monitors.md:187:You arrange the cooldown manager in its settings window, which WowVision makes accessible. Open it with `/cdm`. The window has the Spells, Auras and Group Buffs tabs, a Search box, a Settings menu, each category with its items, the Layout dropdown and a Revert Changes button. Press Enter on an item to pick it up and Enter on another item, or on the empty slot of a category, to drop it there. Press Backspace on an item for its menu (alerts, move to another category). Changes made with the keyboard take effect after the interface reloads, because the game rebuilds its on-screen cooldown data from them. The window tells you so the first time and offers a Reload Interface button at its end (or type `/reload`); when you close the window after such changes it reminds you again. The items are read with their name, "Unlearned" for spells you have not learned, and the number of alerts they have (for example "1 alert"). Categories can be collapsed and expanded with Enter on their heading. On the Group Buffs tab the buffs sit in a Shown and a Hidden section: Enter picks a buff up, and Enter on a buff in the other section moves it there. Choosing an alert in the item's menu (Add New Alert, or editing an existing one) opens the game's alert editor as a window of its own, called Alert Editor: the spell, one dropdown for each choice, and the button that adds or saves the alert, then Close.
docs/src/user/en\information\monitors.md:189:You rarely need the game's alerts, though: WowVision's monitors do everything they do, and do it better. Each state of a rule has its own sound and its own spoken message, in your own speech settings, and you can change both at any time. Spell Cooldowns also works for any spell, not only those in the cooldown manager. The game's alert editor is there if you want it, but start with a monitor.
docs/src/user/en\information\monitors.md:225:The key is unbound by default. You find it under Monitors in the keybinding window, see [Keybindings](../basics/keybindings.md).
docs/src/user/en\information\monitors.md:280:The key is unbound by default. You find it under Monitors in the keybinding window.
docs/src/user/en\information\monitors.md:304:Bind them in the keybinding window, see [Keybindings](../basics/keybindings.md). Toggle All Monitors works as a mute: press it once to silence all monitors and again to turn them all back on. Monitors you had switched off one by one are turned on as well.
docs/src/user/en\information\monitors.md:326:6. Open Expiring, then TTS Alert. Turn Enabled on. Set Message to "Moonfire expiring".
docs/src/user/en\information\monitors.md:327:7. Go back and open Missing, then TTS Alert. Turn Enabled on. Set Message to "Moonfire gone".
docs/src/user/en\information\monitors.md:336:3. Open Ready, then TTS Alert, turn it on and set Message to "Starfire ready".
docs/src/user/en\information\monitors.md:344:3. Open Ready, TTS Alert, turn on Enabled.
docs/src/user/en\information\monitors.md:346:When Barkskin comes back you hear "Barkskin Ready", also in combat. Add the On Cooldown TTS alert if you also want "Barkskin On Cooldown" when you use it. Bind Speak Tracked Cooldowns to hear the state and the time left on demand.
docs/src/user/en\information\monitors.md:353:4. Turn on the TTS Alert of Missing and Pandemic.
docs/src/user/en\information\monitors.md:364:- If an alert never fires, check in this order: the monitor is Enabled; the rule is Enabled; the Spell is set; the state's Sound Alert or TTS Alert is Enabled (they are off by default); and for the sound, a Sound has been chosen.
docs/src/user/en\getting-started\introduction.md:17:It guides you through the world. The navigation window (F10) plans routes along a network of waypoints and leads you there with sound beacons; the scanner (F9) lists quest givers, quest objectives and other things nearby; the minimap scan finds herbs and ore. See [Navigation and Routes](../world/navigation.md), [Scanner](../world/scanner.md) and [Minimap](../world/minimap.md).
docs/src/user/en\getting-started\introduction.md:44:- [Basics](../basics/index.md) — the interface, tooltips, speech, the settings menu, alerts and keybindings. Read these first.
docs/src/user/en\getting-started\introduction.md:57:- Every key mentioned here is the default. You can change all of them; see [Keybindings](../basics/keybindings.md).
docs/src/user/en\reference\index.md:5:- [Keybinding Reference](keybindings.md) — every WowVision binding with its default key
docs/src/user/en\getting-started\managing.md:11:| WowVisionGlobalDB | Most settings, alerts, key bindings, buffers and monitors by default | The whole account |
docs/src/user/en\getting-started\managing.md:21:By default most settings are shared by all your characters. You can make one module's settings belong to a single character instead: in the WowVision menu, put the focus on the module, press Shift+F10 and choose Scope. The key bindings (including controller buttons) are shared the same way, and have their own Scope entry on the Bindings button. See [The WowVision Menu and Settings](../basics/menu-and-settings.md) and [Keybindings](../basics/keybindings.md).
docs/src/user/en\reference\versions.md:20:- Speech settings, alerts, the WowVision menu, keybindings: [Basics](../basics/index.md)
docs/src/user/en\reference\versions.md:31:- Mailbox inbox, trade window, loot roll keys: [Loot, Trade and Mail](../windows/loot-trade-mail.md)
docs/src/user/en\reference\glossary.md:13:Beacon — A sound placed in the game world at a waypoint or destination. You hear where it is from its direction and pitch and walk towards it. See [Navigation and Routes](../world/navigation.md).
docs/src/user/en\reference\glossary.md:15:Binding — An action that can be put on a key, such as Next Buffer Item. A binding can have several keys and, on Retail and WoW: Forever, controller buttons. See [Keybindings](../basics/keybindings.md).
docs/src/user/en\reference\glossary.md:29:Focus target (virtual focus) — One of five targets WowVision stores by name (/focus1 to /focus5) that you can target again with a key. Independent of the game's own focus. See [Targeting](../world/targeting.md).
docs/src/user/en\reference\glossary.md:41:Output — How an alert is delivered: TTS Alert (spoken by text to speech), Sound Alert (a sound file), Voice Alert (a recording from a voice pack). See [Alerts](../basics/alerts.md).
docs/src/user/en\reference\glossary.md:61:Unbound — A binding that exists but has no key until you give it one in the keybinding window. See [Keybinding Reference](keybindings.md).
docs/src/user/en\reference\glossary.md:111:In the [Keybinding Reference](../reference/keybindings.md), and in the game in the keybinding window: WowVision menu, UI, Bindings.
docs/src/user/en\reference\keybindings.md:1:# Keybinding Reference
docs/src/user/en\reference\keybindings.md:3:This page lists every WowVision keybinding with its default key. You can change any of them in the keybinding window: open the WowVision menu (Shift+F1), go to UI and choose Bindings. How the keybinding window works is explained in [Keybindings](../basics/keybindings.md).
docs/src/user/en\reference\keybindings.md:5:"Unbound" means the binding exists but has no key until you give it one.
docs/src/user/en\reference\keybindings.md:95:| Cancel Navigation | Shift+F12 | Stops the active route or beacon |
docs/src/user/en\reference\keybindings.md:100:| Direct to the Nearest Unvisited of the Same Name | Unbound | The same as a straight beacon instead of a route |
docs/src/user/en\reference\keybindings.md:104:Alt Gr is not a binding: it is read directly, stays silent while you type in an edit box, and can be turned off with the Announce Distance with Alt Gr setting in the Maps module. See [Navigation and Routes](../world/navigation.md) and [WowNav](../world/wownav.md).
docs/src/user/en\reference\keybindings.md:157:On Retail and WoW: Forever every binding can also have controller buttons. The defaults are listed in [Controller](../controller.md).
docs/src/user/en\reference\keybindings.md:161:These are not extra bindings. They are the window keys from the first sections, doing a job that belongs to one window. Changing the binding changes the key everywhere.
docs/src/user/en\reference\keybindings.md:183:Some WowVision bindings do the same job as keys of Sku, the older accessibility addon. While Sku is loaded, WowVision does not activate these bindings at all and leaves the keys to Sku:
docs/src/user/en\windows\social.md:347:Alt+T opens the menu of your current target, the one a right click on the target's portrait opens: whisper, invite and the rest. With yourself targeted it offers Reset All Instances, loot and dungeon difficulty. With no target nothing opens. It is bound by default and can be changed in the keybinding window. It is described with the other targeting keys in [Targeting](../world/targeting.md).
docs/src/user/en\getting-started\manual-installation.md:82:The sounds, beacon tones and recorded voices (voice packs) are inside the WowVision folder too. If you ever see a "missing sound" problem, the cause is a damaged or partial extraction: delete the folder and extract the zip again.
docs/src/user/en\getting-started\quick-start.md:80:All keys are listed in the [Keybinding Reference](../reference/keybindings.md), and you can change any of them: see [Keybindings](../basics/keybindings.md).
docs/src/user/en\windows\spells-and-talents.md:11:| Mists of Pandaria (Mists) | Spell pages, Professions, Core Abilities and What Has Changed tabs. Spell flyouts. | Specialization and tier talents. The Glyphs page is not read yet. |
docs/src/user/en\windows\spells-and-talents.md:13:| WoW: Forever | Category tabs, search, paged spell grid. Spell flyouts. | The class talent tree with one stop per specialization, apply, undo and reset. |
docs/src/user/en\windows\spells-and-talents.md:25:| \\ or Ctrl+D | Drag: picks the spell or talent up onto your cursor so you can put it on an action bar (not on the TBC talent grid, see below). See [Action bars](action-bars.md). |
docs/src/user/en\windows\spells-and-talents.md:72:### Spell flyouts
docs/src/user/en\windows\spells-and-talents.md:74:Some abilities (mage teleports and portals, hunter pet calls, warlock summons) open a flyout. WowVision opens it as a window named Spell Flyout, one stop with a button per spell. Enter casts the spell, and Escape closes the flyout.
docs/src/user/en\windows\spells-and-talents.md:110:4. Talent Grid: the talents of the open tree, read top to bottom and left to right. Each reads its name and rank: "Improved Heroic Strike rank 2/3". Only talents the game shows are listed.
docs/src/user/en\windows\spells-and-talents.md:113:Enter on a talent spends a point on it at once and the new rank is spoken ("rank 3/3"). The game rules apply: you need the points spent above and the prerequisite talent. Press Space for the tooltip, which says what is missing and what the next rank does. To pick up a talent that is an active ability and put it on your bars, open the context menu with Shift+F10 and choose Drag; the drag key itself does not work on the TBC talent grid.
docs/src/user/en\windows\spells-and-talents.md:115:### Example: spend your first points in a Warrior tree
docs/src/user/en\windows\spells-and-talents.md:118:2. Press Tab past the tabs and the points line until you hear "Talent Grid".
docs/src/user/en\windows\spells-and-talents.md:187:| Enter | Buys one rank. |
docs/src/user/en\windows\system.md:29:3. Categories: the category list, such as Gameplay, Controls, Graphics, Audio, Interface, Social and Keybindings. Group headers are read as "Name, Separator". Enter on a category shows its settings.
docs/src/user/en\windows\system.md:61:### Keybindings
docs/src/user/en\windows\system.md:63:The Keybindings category lists the game's own key bindings, in sections.
docs/src/user/en\windows\system.md:67:- Each binding is a pair of buttons, named for the action, for example "Jump". Each button reads the key it holds, or "Not Bound". Use Left and Right to choose between the two.
docs/src/user/en\windows\system.md:68:- Enter on a button starts rebinding. Press the key (or key combination) you want. When it works, WowVision says "Rebinding successful".
docs/src/user/en\windows\system.md:71:This changes the game's bindings. WowVision's own keys (the ones that move you through windows) are changed in WowVision's keybinding window instead. See [Keybindings](../basics/keybindings.md).
docs/src/user/en\getting-started\sku.md:7:When the game loads WowVision, it checks which addons are already loaded. If Sku is among them, every window, key binding and command marked as "belongs to Sku" is switched off for that session: the window simply never opens in WowVision, the key is not taken over, the command is not registered. The decision is made once at login, so enabling or disabling Sku needs a reload (/reload) or a new login to take effect. Nothing is deleted: your WowVision settings and key bindings for these features stay as they are and come back as soon as Sku is off.
docs/src/user/en\getting-started\sku.md:55:- Cooldown manager window and its alert editors.
docs/src/user/en\getting-started\troubleshooting.md:56:1. You are in combat. Override bindings cannot change during combat, so WowVision hands all window keys back to the game when a fight starts and takes them again afterwards. Global keys keep working. See [Combat](../world/combat.md).
docs/src/user/en\getting-started\troubleshooting.md:60:5. Another addon or a game binding has the same key. Open the WowVision menu (Shift+F1), then UI, then Bindings, and look at what the binding is set to. When you add a key that another WowVision binding already uses, you are asked "This input conflicts with ... Replace?". Keys held by other addons or by the game are not checked, so try a different key. See [Keybindings](../basics/keybindings.md).
docs/src/user/en\getting-started\troubleshooting.md:62:7. You rebound or removed the key. Some bindings need at least one key and cannot be emptied, but most can. The [keybinding reference](../reference/keybindings.md) lists the defaults.
docs/src/user/en\getting-started\troubleshooting.md:83:- Type /disableaddon sku. This turns off Sku and its audio and beacon helper addons and reloads the interface. /enableaddon sku turns them back on.
docs/src/user/en\getting-started\troubleshooting.md:87:Your WowVision settings and key bindings for those features are not deleted while Sku runs; they come back as soon as Sku is off.
docs/src/user/en\getting-started\troubleshooting.md:94:2. They belong to another scope. By default most settings are shared by all characters (account scope). If you moved a module to a single character, its settings only show on that character. Put the focus on the module in the WowVision menu, press Shift+F10 and look at Scope. Key bindings and a few other things are always per character. See [The WowVision Menu and Settings](../basics/menu-and-settings.md).

