---docs/src/user/en/getting-started/manual-installation.md:80-84---
80:WowVision comes with text in English, German and French; the language of your game client decides which one is used, and any other client language falls back to English.
81:
82:The sounds, beacon tones and recorded voices (voice packs) are inside the WowVision folder too. If you ever see a "missing sound" problem, the cause is a damaged or partial extraction: delete the folder and extract the zip again.
83:
84:## Updating by hand
---docs/src/user/en/information/monitors.md:185-190---
185:- The cooldown manager must be showing the aura. A cooldown manager row that you have hidden, or set to appear only in combat, reads as empty while it is hidden, and your rule will report Missing. If an alert plays at the wrong time, check that the rows are shown.
186:
187:You arrange the cooldown manager in its settings window, which WowVision makes accessible. Open it with `/cdm`. The window has the Spells, Auras and Group Buffs tabs, a Search box, a Settings menu, each category with its items, the Layout dropdown and a Revert Changes button. Press Enter on an item to pick it up and Enter on another item, or on the empty slot of a category, to drop it there. Press Backspace on an item for its menu (alerts, move to another category). Changes made with the keyboard take effect after the interface reloads, because the game rebuilds its on-screen cooldown data from them. The window tells you so the first time and offers a Reload Interface button at its end (or type `/reload`); when you close the window after such changes it reminds you again. The items are read with their name, "Unlearned" for spells you have not learned, and the number of alerts they have (for example "1 alert"). Categories can be collapsed and expanded with Enter on their heading. On the Group Buffs tab the buffs sit in a Shown and a Hidden section: Enter picks a buff up, and Enter on a buff in the other section moves it there. Choosing an alert in the item's menu (Add New Alert, or editing an existing one) opens the game's alert editor as a window of its own, called Alert Editor: the spell, one dropdown for each choice, and the button that adds or saves the alert, then Close.
188:
189:You rarely need the game's alerts, though: WowVision's monitors do everything they do, and do it better. Each state of a rule has its own sound and its own spoken message, in your own speech settings, and you can change both at any time. Spell Cooldowns also works for any spell, not only those in the cooldown manager. The game's alert editor is there if you want it, but start with a monitor.
190:
---docs/src/user/en/reference/glossary.md:39-43---
39:Monitor — Something that watches the game for you and fires alerts when a state changes, for example a debuff falling off your target. Made of rules. See [Monitors](../information/monitors.md).
40:
41:Output — How an alert is delivered: TTS Alert (spoken by text to speech), Sound Alert (a sound file), Voice Alert (a recording from a voice pack). See [Alerts](../basics/alerts.md).
42:
43:Pandemic window — The last part of a damage-over-time effect's duration in which refreshing it does not lose any time. Monitors can alert when an aura enters this window.
---docs/src/user/en/reference/keybindings.md:1-12---
1:# Keybinding Reference
2:
3:This page lists every WowVision keybinding with its default key. You can change any of them in the keybinding window: open the WowVision menu (Shift+F1), go to UI and choose Bindings. How the keybinding window works is explained in [Keybindings](../basics/keybindings.md).
4:
5:"Unbound" means the binding exists but has no key until you give it one.
6:
7:## Two kinds of keys
8:
9:- Window keys only work while a WowVision window is open (bags, a merchant, the WowVision menu and so on). When no window is open, the same keys do whatever you bound them to in the game. Arrow keys, Tab, Enter, Backspace, Escape, Space, Home, End, Page Up, Page Down and the context menu key are window keys. On game windows Escape stays with the game, which closes its own windows; WowVision uses it on its own screens (see [Using the Interface](../basics/interface.md)).
10:- Global keys work at any time, with or without a window: buffers, navigation, targeting, the loot roll keys, Destroy Cursor Item and the keys that open WowVision windows.
11:
12:In combat, WowVision hands the window keys back to the game (see [Combat](../world/combat.md)). The global keys keep working.
---docs/src/user/en/reference/keybindings.md:102-106---
102:| New WowNav Route Around a Blocked Spot | Unbound | (WoW: Forever) Plans a new route that avoids the spot where you are stuck |
103:
104:Alt Gr is not a binding: it is read directly, stays silent while you type in an edit box, and can be turned off with the Announce Distance with Alt Gr setting in the Maps module. See [Navigation and Routes](../world/navigation.md) and [WowNav](../world/wownav.md).
105:
106:## Minimap
---docs/src/user/en/reference/keybindings.md:155-185---
155:| Close (Controller) | Unbound (no keyboard key) | Gives the controller's close button the game's own Escape on windows where Escape stays with the game |
156:
157:On Retail and WoW: Forever every binding can also have controller buttons. The defaults are listed in [Controller](../controller.md).
158:
159:## Keys inside specific windows
160:
161:These are not extra bindings. They are the window keys from the first sections, doing a job that belongs to one window. Changing the binding changes the key everywhere.
162:
163:| Where | Key | What it does |
164:|---|---|---|
165:| Chat window (Shift+F3), message list | Up, Down, Home, End | Move between messages |
166:| Chat window, message list | Left, Right | Switch to the previous or next chat tab |
167:| Chat window, message list | Ctrl+Left, Ctrl+Right | Move between the clickable parts of a message (channel, sender, links) |
168:| Chat window, message list | Enter, Backspace, Shift+Enter, Space | Left click, right click, shift click and read the tooltip of the selected part |
169:| Navigation window (F10), Destinations list | Up, Down, Home, End | Walk the long destination list; Enter starts the route |
170:| Navigation window, Search box | Enter, Tab, Escape | Enter and Tab apply the text, Escape closes the box without changing the search |
171:| Scanner (F9) | Right, Left, Enter | Open or step into a group, close it or step out, guide you to the entry |
172:| Scanner | Backspace | Opens the extra text of an entry, such as a quest's objective |
173:| Scanner | Tab | Jumps between the list and the Refresh button |
174:| Action bars (Shift+F4), bags and other slot windows | \\ or Ctrl+D, then Delete | Pick up a slot onto the cursor, then clear or destroy it |
175:| Friends, ignore, who and guild lists (Classic Era, TBC and Mists) | Page Up, Page Down | Scroll the list a page; Home and End do nothing there |
176:| WowVision menu, context menus, dropdown menus | Letters | Jump to the next entry that starts with the letter. Can be switched off with the Letter Navigation in Menus setting |
177:| Edit boxes | Enter, Tab, Escape | Enter puts the cursor in a game box and accepts a WowVision box; Tab moves on; Escape cancels a WowVision box |
178:
179:See [Chat](../windows/chat.md), [Navigation and Routes](../world/navigation.md), [Scanner](../world/scanner.md), [Action Bars](../windows/action-bars.md) and [Using the Interface](../basics/interface.md).
180:
181:## When Sku is installed
182:
183:Some WowVision bindings do the same job as keys of Sku, the older accessibility addon. While Sku is loaded, WowVision does not activate these bindings at all and leaves the keys to Sku:
184:
185:- the four tooltip line keys (Shift+Up, Shift+Down, Shift+Left, Shift+Right)
---docs/src/user/en/reference/versions.md:100-106---
100:| Pets, toys, heirlooms, appearances | No | No | No | No | Yes |
101:| Legacy (reward track, challenges, trees) | No | No | No | No | Yes |
102:| Group finder | No | Yes | No | Yes (Dungeon Finder; other tabs not yet) | Yes |
103:| Pet stable | No | Yes | No | No | No |
104:| Auction house | No | Yes (with price database and full scan) | Yes | Yes | Yes |
105:
106:See [Collections](../windows/collections.md), [Group Finder](../windows/group-finder.md) and [Auction House](../windows/auction-house.md).
---docs/src/user/en/windows/action-bars.md:13-17---
13:
14:You cannot navigate the window while you are in combat. WowVision gives its keys back to the game during combat so that your own key bindings keep working, and takes them again when combat ends. Use your normal bindings for abilities in a fight.
15:
16:## How the window is laid out
17:
---docs/src/user/en/windows/action-bars.md:57-61---
57:
58:The label updates while you are standing on it. If you pick a spell up from a slot, it immediately reads "Empty". Spells, items, macros, mounts, battle pets, equipment sets and flyouts are all read by name. Rarely a slot reads "Unknown", followed by technical details such as an id and a type; that is something the addon cannot name yet. A slot whose button the game has switched off says "Disabled" after its name.
59:
60:### Pet Bar
61:
---docs/src/user/en/windows/character.md:80-86---
80:2. New Set.
81:3. Once a set is selected, one stop each for Equip, Save, Edit Set and Delete. Equip puts the selected set on. Save stores what you wear now into it. Edit Set opens the name and icon window. Delete asks the game for a confirmation popup, which you answer like any [popup](system.md).
82:
83:New Set and Edit Set open the set window as a window of its own: a name edit box, an icon type dropdown, an Icon number you change with Left and Right (it reads "3 of 120"; Enter lets you type a number) and the Okay and Cancel buttons. The window opens with the cursor in the name box cleared, so you can use the arrow keys at once; Enter on the name box puts you back into typing.
84:
85:The sidebar also has a Sidebar Tabs stop (a row of tabs: Left/Right and Enter) and a button that collapses or expands the right panel. When the panel is collapsed only the equipment list and the toggle button are read. The pet stats view is called Pet and lists the stats of your pet.
86:
---docs/src/user/en/windows/character.md:163-167---
163:
164:On WoW: Forever a currency row also reads "selected" when chosen. The chosen currency has a Details stop with two check boxes, one that marks the currency as unused (it moves out of the main list) and one that shows it on your backpack bar, plus a transfer button for currencies you can move between your characters. The check boxes read with the game's own wording.
165:
166:## Statistics (WoW: Forever only)
167:
---docs/src/user/en/windows/chat.md:181-185---
181:1. Categories: a list of buttons, such as the chat settings, the channel settings and the other message groups. Enter opens one.
182:2. Chat settings, Channel settings and further groups of check boxes: each group is named by its heading and holds the check boxes of the messages (Say, Yell, Guild, Whisper, Loot, and so on). Enter turns a check box on or off.
183:3. Channels: the list of global channels (General, Trade and so on), where Enter joins or leaves a channel for this tab.
184:
185:Which groups you see depends on the category you picked. Escape closes the window.
---docs/src/user/en/windows/collections.md:194-212---
194:|---|---|
195:| Enter | Buys a rank (for a choice talent: opens its options) |
196:| Backspace | Refunds a rank |
197:| Space | Reads the talent's full tooltip |
198:| \\ | Picks up an active ability for an action bar |
199:| Shift+F10 | Context menu: Learn, Unlearn, Drag and "Link (talent) in Chat" |
200:
201:Buying is staged: nothing is learned until you press Apply. The apply button reads "Unsaved Changes" while changes are waiting. Undo Changes drops them, and Reset Tree refunds the whole tree (also staged until Apply). Choice talents open the game's option list; Enter picks an option, Backspace on one unlearns it, and Escape returns to the tree. The same talent tree reader is used for the class talents; see [Spells and talents](./spells-and-talents.md).
202:
203:### Example: spend Legacy points on a tree
204:
205:1. Open the Legacy window with the Toggle Legacy key.
206:2. On the Tabs row, move to the tree page and press Enter.
207:3. Tab to Trees and choose the tree you want.
208:4. Tab to Talents. Use Down to reach a row and Right to find a talent that reads "Available".
209:5. Press Enter to buy a rank. Repeat for other talents.
210:6. Tab to the Apply button (it reads "Unsaved Changes") and press Enter.
211:
212:## What is not read yet
---docs/src/user/en/windows/loot-trade-mail.md:74-79---
74:The stops, in order:
75:
76:1. Tabs: Inbox and Send Mail. The one you are on says "selected". Left and Right move between them, and Enter switches.
77:2. Open All: a button that takes everything from your inbox, when the game shows it.
78:3. Inbox: your letters on this page.
79:4. Previous Page and Next Page: only when there are more letters than fit.
---docs/src/user/en/windows/loot-trade-mail.md:110-114---
110:1. Open the mailbox and press Enter on "From: Auction House, Subject: Auction successful".
111:2. Tab to the letter. Listen to the invoice.
112:3. Down to the money attachment and press Enter. The gold is in your purse.
113:4. Tab to Delete (or Close) and press Enter.
114:
---docs/src/user/en/windows/spells-and-talents.md:108-117---
108:2. Tabs: the three talent trees, named by the game ("Arms", "Fury", "Protection"). The open one reads "selected".
109:3. A line with your unspent talent points, as the game words it.
110:4. Talent Grid: the talents of the open tree, read top to bottom and left to right. Each reads its name and rank: "Improved Heroic Strike rank 2/3". Only talents the game shows are listed.
111:5. Close.
112:
113:Enter on a talent spends a point on it at once and the new rank is spoken ("rank 3/3"). The game rules apply: you need the points spent above and the prerequisite talent. Press Space for the tooltip, which says what is missing and what the next rank does. To pick up a talent that is an active ability and put it on your bars, open the context menu with Shift+F10 and choose Drag; the drag key itself does not work on the TBC talent grid.
114:
115:### Example: spend your first points in a Warrior tree
116:
117:1. Press N. You land on the tabs stop (or on the specialization row if you have one).
---docs/src/user/en/world/combat.md:29-33---
29:(Retail and WoW: Forever only) Blizzard hides a number of game values from addons, especially in combat and in instances. These are the ones that you notice in WowVision:
30:
31:- Your target's health cannot be read, so there is no target health monitor. Use the game's own Combat Audio Alerts instead, in its Accessibility options: they can say your target's health, in combat too. See [Targeting](targeting.md).
32:- Your movement speed is hidden in combat, so wall detection can only notice that you are standing still, not that you are slowed ([Movement](movement.md)).
33:- Raid markers are spoken as numbers.
---docs/src/user/en/world/movement.md:19-23---
19:
20:The Compass module watches your character and speaks when something about your surroundings changes. Each of its entries is an alert (see [Alerts](../basics/alerts.md)): it has an Enabled switch and one or more outputs, usually TTS Alert (speech) and Sound Alert.
21:
22:| Alert | What triggers it | Default output |
23:|-------|------------------|----------------|
---docs/src/user/en/world/targeting.md:1-6---
1:# Targeting
2:
3:WowVision tells you what you target, lets you keep up to five "virtual focus" targets on keys, can switch the game's soft targeting on and off, and opens the target's menu with a single key. This page covers all of it: the announcements, the keys, and the settings.
4:
5:All targeting settings live in the WowVision menu (type /wv) under Targeting. Targeting works the same on every supported game version, apart from the notes marked for Retail and WoW: Forever. Soft targeting needs support from the game itself; if your version lacks one of its settings, /wv soft lists it as "not available".
6:
---docs/src/user/en/world/targeting.md:56-60---
56:
57:(Retail and WoW: Forever) There is no Health Monitor here, because the game hides a target's health from addons in combat, just when you need it. Use the game's own Combat Audio Alerts instead, in its Accessibility options: they can say your target's health, in combat too.
58:
59:Example: you want a quieter fight, so you turn off health readouts.
60:
---docs/src/user/en/world/targeting.md:101-105---
101:
102:Each type is an alert in the Targeting menu named Soft Target Enemy, Soft Target Friend and Soft Target Interact. The alert is off by default; its Enabled setting is what the key flips. Under it you find TTS Alert (spoken, interrupting by default) and Sound Alert, both on by default once the alert is on. You can pick a different sound with the Sound setting of the sound output.
103:
104:### Arc and range
105:
---docs/src/user/en/world/targeting.md:164-170---
164:### Example: three bosses in a raid
165:
166:1. Target the first boss and type /focus1.
167:2. Target the second boss and type /focus2, then the third and /focus3.
168:3. In the keybinding window assign Alt+1, Alt+2 and Alt+3 to Target Focus 1, 2 and 3.
169:4. In the fight, Alt+1 targets the first boss and WowVision reads it as it would any target change.
170:

